1) To start a new research project, you should go on GitHub to create a new repo.
2) Then, clone the GitHub repo to your local computer (either your local working directory or MaloneLab Server, whichever you prefer)
3) From our debugging today, it seems like the best method is to just clone with your Terminal. However, I think the RStudio method will
work just as well (RStudio method: New Project -> Version Control -> Git -> paste url of your GitHub repo inside the prompt)
4) Check your local repo's connection to GitHub by running "git remote -v" in the Terminal (make sure you're inside the local repo too)
5) If it says "fatal: detected dubious ownership in repository at ...", follow their directions to add a safe directory
(the directions may look something like this: "git config --global --add safe.directory ...")
6) After you run this command, double-check again by running "git remote -v". This should now point to the GitHub repo as the remote repo.
7) Restart RStudio just in case. 
8) After restarting, you should now see a "Git" pane in the upper right hand corner.
9) Proceed with the usual Git workflow by staging (checking the checkboxes) -> writing commit message -> press "Commit button" -> pull -> push
10) Success! 